<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ingrian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a>–<a href="/years/1617">1617</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ingrian War was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> during and after Russia's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>. Swedish troops occupied Novgorod in 1611, and Sweden supported a Swedish candidate for the Russian throne. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stolbovo">Treaty of Stolbovo</a> of 1617 returned Novgorod but gave Sweden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kexholm">Kexholm</a>, cutting Russia off from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ingrian-war.jpg" alt="Ingrian War" width="320" height="288" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johan Hammer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slaget_vid_Novgorod_1611_(Johan_Hammer)_-_Nationalmuseum_-_35876.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Time of Troubles, Tsar Vasily IV was besieged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> by supporters of False Dmitry II. Driven to despair by Polish intervention, he allied with King Charles IX of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, who was also at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Vyborg</a> of 1609 the tsar promised to cede the Korela Fortress to Sweden and renounced all Russian claims on the Baltic coast. The Swedish commander Jacob De la Gardie joined the Russian commander Mikhail Skopin-Shuisky in the De la Gardie campaign.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s involvement gave King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> a pretext to declare war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. After Skopin-Shuisky died suddenly, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> defeated the combined Russo-Swedish force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-klushino">Battle of Klushino</a> on 4 July 1610. The tsar was deposed by the Seven Boyars and the Poles occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-kremlin">Moscow Kremlin</a>. De la Gardie then sought to control the Russian north-west so that the Poles could not threaten Sweden's Baltic territories.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In July 1611 De la Gardie captured Novgorod and compelled its people to acknowledge the Swedish king as their patron and to choose one of his sons, Carl Filip or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a>, as monarch. Gustavus Adolphus then came to the Swedish throne and pursued his brother's claim to the Russian crown, even after a patriotic uprising expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1612 and Mikhail Romanov was elected tsar.</p><p>In 1613 the Swedes besieged Tikhvin but were repelled, and a Russian counteroffensive failed to retake Novgorod. In 1614 the Swedes captured Gdov, and thousands of soldiers freed by the peace of Knäred were shipped over. The following year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, which the Russian generals Morozov and Buturlin defended. Negotiations began on 9 March 1617.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stolbovo">Treaty of Stolbovo</a> of 1617 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivangorod">Ivangorod</a>, Jama, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koporye">Koporye</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oreshek-fortress">Nöteborg</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kexholm">Kexholm</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, while restoring Novgorod and Gdov. Russia renounced claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, owed 20,000 rubles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> and was denied access to the Baltic for about a century, which raised the importance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Arkhangelsk</a> for trade. Sweden recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia">Michael Romanov</a> as tsar.</p><p>Most sources regard the war as a Swedish victory. The treaty was considered humiliating in the Soviet era, but recent Russian historiography notes that it freed forces to face <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. German Zamyatin argues that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> suffered a diplomatic defeat in failing to secure Novgorod and Gdov, and Gennady Kovalenko calls it a mutually beneficial compromise.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ingrian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Stolbovo ended the <b>Ingrian War</b> between Sweden and Russia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-27">27 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
